we're happy to report that 10 dw  correspondents working in afghanistan  have now been able to leave the country  following its takeover by the thalibon  the correspondents entered neighboring  pakistan earlier today this group  includes dw's sole female correspondent  in afghanistan the situation for  journalists on the ground has become  increasingly dangerous with reports of  intimidation and violence at the hands  of the taliban joined now by one of the  dw correspondents marjana sadat who  joins me now from the phone from  pakistan majana thank you for taking the  time to speak with us i want to start by  asking you you were able to leave  afghanistan what are your feelings right  now  thank you for having me on your program  uh as you know uh there was no hope for  the future anymore in afghanistan but at  on that time i decided to uh  be on this situation and work and want  to speak to write and uh for portraying  most of today's narratives even it's a  was even it was uh done by secretly but  uh we decided to write about the uh  material of the land and to portray of  the displacement of our people and our  generation there and it's it was for uh  our history uh so i i didn't experience  those uh dark days and you know the 15  years back was the best days of my life  and the other woman's in afghanistan i  studied we went to universities worked  with the  most prestigious international media and  i unlike me  many women in various fields of works  and those days were just our golden days  of our lifetime and it's finished just  three weeks ago when the taliban took  over the afghanistan marjana you see the  golden days of your lives basically  taken away that day when the thalibon  took kabul what were your feelings that  day when you saw that was taking place  uh actually on that day uh i was uh i  was outside outside the home and i  wanted to go and record the reports from  kabul university i was in german  department and public university my  colleague called me and told me that  taliban is into  entertaining kabul and you need to uh  come to office on the first time i came  down uh they are coming down or on our  block and asked the guard who is living  here in this block i was looking at them  throughout the window of my apartment  and i just shocked because i didn't  experience of  the taliban regime uh before so um now  uh as in the past i can't  freely hold my camera and take pictures  and videos from the uh of the cobble  roads interview with the people freely  and like me any or or other journalists  as well uh they cannot  do their uh job like freely and uh go to  a roots and without any fear and they're  in kabul  marjana thank you we're relieved to hear  that you're safe and sound and thank you  again for speaking with us  the u.s is preparing to mark the 20th  anniversary of the 9 11 attacks the  terrorist strikes using hijacked  airliners killed nearly 3 000 people  events are taking place across the  country students at this new jersey  school have installed 2977  flags on the front lawn that's one flag  for each person killed when the jets hit  the world trade center in new york and  the pentagon in washington a fourth  plane crashed in a field in pennsylvania  the u.n development agency has warned  that afghanistan could soon be on the  brink of economic and social collapse if  no effort is made to support the economy  they say that takeover by the taliban  has put more than 20 years of gains at  risk and that large parts of the country  could be plunged into poverty by the  middle of next year the comments come as  the international community faces off  over whether to unfreeze billions of  dollars of funds belonging to  afghanistan and currently held in the  united states  some say the risk of not doing so could  leave millions hungry and generate new  waves of refugees but the u.s says it  has conditions  if a new afghan government upholds its  commitments and  obligations brings greater stability to  the country and region  demonstrates real inclusion  and protects the gains of the past two  decades  will work with it  but we will not do that on the basis of  trust or faith alone  the taliban seeks international  legitimacy and support  our message is simple  any legitimacy and support will have to  be earned  but pakistan is urging a different  approach its foreign minister called for  the assets to be unfrozen and for the  international community to step in to  avert a humanitarian crisis in  afghanistan calling it a collective  responsibility  but what we are saying is okay  if you're not ready  for immediate uh  economic aid or development  fine  but  do not take steps  that would uh  you know lead to  an economic collapse  of afghanistan  that will not help  that will not help anyone  and pakistan for its part has already  begun sending aid to afghanistan the  country delivered a first shipment to  kabul of 30 tons of supplies including  food medical equipment and medicine the  pakistani government says deliveries  will continue in the coming days and in  the next round relief planes will land  in kandahar herat and khost provinces  but pakistan's involvement in  afghanistan extends far beyond aid the 9  11 terror attacks meant it was to play a  pivotal role in u.s operations and  policy in afghanistan a prime reason for  that was pakistan's support for the  taliban it formed the basis of  cooperation and confrontation with the  united states we'll talk about that in  detail in a moment but here's a quick  summary of how that relationship has  played out over the last 20 years  in the wake of the 9 11 attacks  pakistan's leader general pervez  musharraf declared pakistan would join  the war worn terror as a us ally  in return for the lifting of sanctions  and billions of dollars in aid pakistan  provided the u.s with a number of  military airports and bases for its  attack on afghanistan  along with other logistical support  but the alliance between the two  countries was always shaky marked by  distrust from both sides  many taliban members had been trained in  religious schools in pakistan and  fundamental islamists still held  considerable power  while helping the u.s militarily  pakistan was also harboring taliban and  al-qaeda terrorists  in 2011 10 years after it launched its  war on terror the us tracked down and  killed the mastermind behind the 9 11  attacks osama bin laden  it turned out that he had been holed up  in a secure compound in pakistan  that led many in the u.s to question how  the world's most wanted terrorist could  have been hiding in plain sight in a  country that was supposedly an ally  but the u.s too did much to draw  pakistan's anger continued drone strikes  on taliban targets in pakistani  territory stoked anti-us sentiment  many pakistanis felt they were a  violation of their sovereignty  and hundreds of pakistani civilians as  well as soldiers were killed by u.s  drones  leading to huge demonstrations  more recently pakistan used its  influence with the taliban to act as a  mediator in peace talks between the u.s  and the islamists  but how frail the bond between pakistan  and the u.s really is became clear after  the u.s troop withdrawal from  afghanistan  pakistan's prime minister imran khan  made no secret of his feelings saying in  afghanistan they have just broken the  chains of slavery  with the u.s mission in afghanistan over  and the taliban now in charge the  relationship between the u.s and  pakistan is set to be redefined  joining me now for more is michael  kugelman he's deputy director of the  asia program at the wilson center in  washington dc michael welcome now since  taking office u.s president joe biden  hasn't yet called pakistan prime  minister imran khan i wonder what that  says about the state of the bilateral  relationship  well i think it's an indication of how  this relationship is is  transactional and in other words the  administration is not going to have a  call between the president and prime  minister khan just for the sake of  having the call i think that you would  get a call between biden and imran khan  only if there's something something  major to discuss whether a new agreement  for instance a new say intelligence  sharing agreement to help with  counter-terrorism activities in  afghanistan or something like that  you know the biden administration may  consider pakistan as a partner to an  extent but certainly not a close ally  and i think that the ideas that suggest  that there really is no reason to have  such a prestigious call so to speak  unless there is something really major  to highlight or to discuss  and why does america view this  relationship to be so transactional  well i'd argue there's nothing wrong  with the transactional relationship  first of all in fact the us has very few  relationships that one would describe as  strategic which i think is the logical  opposite of a transactional relationship  uh certainly the us has strategic  relationships with some of its closest  allies such as the uk and israel and  australia but uh most of its  relationships are transactional i think  with pakistan there's just a lot of  divergent interest and there have been  for quite some time  you look back over the last 20 years you  know clearly there's a lot of  disagreement when it comes first of all  to classifying what constitutes a threat  and a terrorist threat obviously  the u.s looks at how pakistan has  treated terrorist organizations  terrorist groups like the haqqani  network  and lashkar-e-taiba it sponsors these  groups the us sees them as as enemies  and the us also believes rightfully so  that pakistan has oftentimes sheltered  the very groups that the u.s wants them  to target and i think given those levels  of divergences and interests and those  levels of mistrust it's very difficult  to think that there would be enough good  will and trust to be able to forge a  deep strategic partnership 