g we  should be watching out for  well if you want to talk about u.s  engagement with the indo-pacific i think  that this has to be framed through the  lens of china and u.s china rivalry and  in fact one of the main reasons why  president biden decided to leave  afghanistan was to be able to focus more  intention on the u.s china competition  so i think that we will see in the  coming weeks and months efforts by the  administration to build on the momentum  of the quad grouping of the us japan  australia and india to  um  develop new areas of cooperation uh  whether that entails the pandemic or  climate change or other things and also  infrastructure i think there's going to  be a  an effort to try to build cooperation on  an infrastructure all in an effort to  push back or should i say to counter  balance um china and i think more  broadly what we're going to see from the  bible administration is to try to  build a greater global consensus um to  push back against china again using  recent developments like a new g7  initiative to develop this new  infrastructure project that would entail  trying to wean countries around the  world away from chinese  infrastructure support that's going to  be hard to do  but i think that's that's the plan now  and so i think that you know the the  administration is increasingly going to  want to have the afghanistan situation  in the rearview mirror even though it's  going to remain engaged to an extent  particularly when you look at  terrorism efforts but the focus will be  on other parts of asia particularly the  east and northeast asia the indo-pacific  we leave it there for the time being but  thank you so much for joining us today  michael kugelman  thank you

